 **Two linearization points, not one.** Introducing or moving a *name* (create, rename) is linearized globally by L2, so it is visible in every zone and never lost or resurrected (section 8.1); a file's *content version* is linearized at its home zone by the commit point. For a new object the global name is published only once its first content version has committed, so no reader anywhere sees a name without content. The exact interleaving is owned by the commit-protocol spec.
 
+## 6.1a Assembled writes (multipart upload)
+
+An **assembled write** is a write whose durable staging outlives any single request or lease
+window — multipart upload today, server-side copy and resumable writes next. It is the first
+consumer of the metadata layer's safety contracts that violates the shape those contracts were
+written for, and it is settled normatively by proposal 0016 (*the multipart commit protocol*).
+Four differences from section 6.1 are load-bearing:
+
+1. **Publication is proved by records and a fence, never a timer.** A single streaming write
+   publishes under *lease liveness*: every chunk must still hold an unexpired `pending:` lease
+   at the commit. A multipart Complete assembles parts committed minutes or days apart, so
+   lease liveness cannot be the proof. Instead the session record is **fenced**
+   (`Open@E → Completing@E+1`, a compare-and-set on its exact bytes) before the part set is
+   read, and every part operation carries a precondition on that record — so the single session
+   precondition proves the whole part set is exactly what the assembler read, at O(1) batch
+   size.
+2. **A third class of durable byte.** Staged bytes are committed to disk and referenced by
+   something that is not an inode. They are named by the **`sidx:`** owned staging entries of an
+   in-flight part and by the **`part:`** records of a committed one, and both are members of the
+   custodian's reference set (section 6.3 / 6.7), so a live upload's bytes are protected
+   explicitly rather than by GC's conservative arm.
+3. **Bounded work for unbounded objects.** An obligation proportional to object size — the
+   fan-out of superseding or deleting a large generation, or of tearing down a 10,000-part
+   session — is installed as **one small `retire:` record**, atomically with the transition that
+   created it, and drained in byte- and operation-budgeted batches. Nothing proportional to
+   object size ever rides inside one transaction or one request.
+4. **Flat or segmented maps.** A published chunk map is one metadata value, so an object whose
+   map exceeds that ceiling is published as **segment records** (`seg:<group-nonce>:<epoch>:<i>`)
+   written ahead of the root, with the root flip as the single publication instant. A single
+   `PutObject` stays flat instead, by choosing its chunk size from the declared `Content-Length`
+   — segmentation needs a session's fence and epoch, which a single PUT has not got.
+
+The client-visible surface is ordinary S3, with three refusals that are normal protocol answers
+rather than errors: *slow down* (too many parts in flight, or too many uploads open),
+*no such upload* (cancelled or already finished), and *too large* (a part, or an assembled
+object, past what this deployment publishes).
+
+**Operational precondition.** Several states of the protocol have no exit but the custodian's
+abandoned-upload reaper — a session whose client walked away, one fenced by a gateway that then
+crashed, a teardown whose draining gateway died. A deployment that exposes multipart without a
+running custodian reaper is therefore misconfigured by construction, and the S3 role makes that
+state operator-visible.
